\title{Exact-Rate Batches, Splitting Trees and Largest-Lag Greedy:\\
Fair Schedules for Arbitrary Positive Rate Profiles}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A rate profile assigns to each of $k$ clients a positive integer rate $r_i$; the period is $R=\sum_{i<k}r_i$, and a schedule is a map $f$ from time slots $\{0,1,2,\dots\}$ to clients with counters $N_i(t)=\#\{u<t: f(u)=i\}$. The discrepancy of client $i$ at time $t$ is $D_i(t)=R\,N_i(t)-r_i t$, and $|D_i(t)|/R$ measures, in whole services, how far the client is from its entitlement. We give a complete analysis of three constructions, valid for \emph{every} client count and \emph{every} positive rate profile, with no arithmetic hypotheses on the rates.

Prefix sums cut one period into pairwise disjoint half-open batches $[P_i,P_{i+1})$ of exactly the prescribed sizes; repeating the tiling gives the \emph{block schedule}, whose counter admits the exact closed form $N_i(t)=\lfloor t/R\rfloor r_i+\min(r_i,(t\bmod R)-P_i)^+$. Every fairness property of the block schedule follows from this identity: exactness at all multiples of the period, the \emph{sharp} two-sided bound $-r_iP_i\le D_i(t)\le r_i(R-P_{i+1})$ with both ends attained, the fairness constant $\max_i r_i(R-r_i)$, and bounded waiting in every window of $R-r_i+1$ slots. With $k\ge 2$ positive rates no schedule is exact at all times; the block schedule is exact precisely at multiples of the period; and every $B$-fair schedule satisfies $B\ge R-r_{f(0)}$, whence round robin is optimal for the uniform profile.

We show that the naive multi-client generalisation of the two-client Bresenham (Beatty) schedule --- differencing floors of scaled prefix sums --- is \emph{not realisable by any schedule} for $k\ge 3$, though it coincides with Bresenham for $k=2$. We then introduce \emph{splitting trees} and prove that the recursive schedule of a well-formed splitting tree $T$ satisfies $|W N_i(t)-w_i t|\le W\dep(T)$, a bound involving only the shape of the tree; balanced trees give, for every $k$ and every positive profile, an explicit schedule of normalised discrepancy at most $\lceil\log_2 k\rceil$. Finally we analyse the online largest-lag greedy rule, prove that it never lets a client run a full period ahead ($D_i(t)\le R-1$) and is $(k-1)(R-1)$-fair, and report a computational refutation of the natural unit-fairness conjecture: the profile $(1,1,1,5,5,5)$ reaches normalised lag $19/18$, and the family $(1^m,c^m)$ pushes this towards $3/2$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} fair scheduling, rate profile, discrepancy, prefix sums, Bresenham/Beatty sequences, splitting trees, largest-lag greedy, apportionment.

\section{Introduction}

A single indivisible resource must be handed out one slot at a time to $k$ competing clients whose entitlements are described by positive integer rates. The question is not whether long-run frequencies can be met --- they trivially can --- but how far from its entitlement a client can be forced at any finite time, and which constructions attain the optimum.

The problem is the common core of several subjects: weighted and deficit round robin in networking; proportional-share CPU scheduling; the product rate variation problem of mixed-model assembly lines; apportionment, where ``staying within quota'' is precisely a discrepancy bound; Beatty sequences and exact covers in number theory; and Bresenham's line algorithm in computer graphics. The unifying object is a sequence over a finite alphabet whose letter frequencies must track prescribed densities uniformly in time.

Our contribution is a complete, hypothesis-free treatment of three constructions: the \textbf{block schedule} built from prefix sums, with an exact closed-form counter and sharp fairness and waiting-time bounds; \textbf{splitting-tree schedules}, achieving normalised discrepancy $\lceil\log_2 k\rceil$ for every profile, together with a proof that the naive nested-floor generalisation of Bresenham is not a schedule at all; and the online \textbf{largest-lag greedy} rule, with a proved one-sided unit bound and a computational refutation of the two-sided one.

\section{Setting and definitions}

\begin{definition}[Rate profile, prefix sums, period]
A \emph{rate profile} on $k\ge 1$ clients is a tuple $r=(r_0,\dots,r_{k-1})$ of positive integers, with prefix sums $P_i=\sum_{j<i}r_j$ $(0\le i\le k)$, $P_0=0$, and \emph{period} $R=P_k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Schedule, counter, discrepancy]
A \emph{schedule} is a function $f:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$; it is a schedule \emph{on $k$ clients} if $f(t)<k$ for all $t$. Its counters are $N^f_i(t)=\#\{u<t: f(u)=i\}$, so $N^f_i(0)=0$, $N^f_i(t+1)=N^f_i(t)+[f(t)=i]$, each $N^f_i$ is non-decreasing, and $\sum_{i<k}N^f_i(t)=t$. The \emph{discrepancy} is
\[
D_i(t)=R\,N^f_i(t)-r_i\,t\in\mathbb{Z},
\]
positive meaning \emph{ahead} (a lead) and negative meaning \emph{behind} (a lag). One has the zero-sum identity $\sum_{i<k}D_i(t)=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fairness, exactness]
A schedule is \emph{$B$-fair} for $(r,k)$ if $|D_i(t)|\le B$ for all $i<k$ and all $t$; its \emph{normalised discrepancy} is $\sup_{i,t}|D_i(t)|/R$, measured in whole services. It is \emph{exact at $t$} if $D_i(t)=0$ for all $i<k$.
\end{definition}

\section{Exact-rate batches and the block schedule}

\begin{definition}[Batches]
For a profile $r$ set $B_i=[P_i,P_{i+1})\cap\mathbb{Z}=\{P_i,\dots,P_{i+1}-1\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Exact-rate disjoint batches]\label{prop:batches}
$|B_i|=r_i$; the batches $B_0,\dots,B_{k-1}$ are pairwise disjoint; and $\bigcup_{i<k}B_i=\{0,1,\dots,R-1\}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$|B_i|=P_{i+1}-P_i=r_i$. For $i<j$, every element of $B_i$ is $<P_{i+1}\le P_j$ by monotonicity of prefix sums, while every element of $B_j$ is $\ge P_j$. The union telescopes to $[P_0,P_k)=[0,R)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Block index, block schedule]
For $0\le s<R$ let $\beta(s)$ be the unique $i<k$ with $s\in B_i$; equivalently $\beta(s)=\#\{i<k: P_{i+1}\le s\}$. The \emph{block schedule} is $\blk(t)=\beta(t\bmod R)$, with counters written $N_i(t)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Counter formula]\label{thm:counter}
Let $R>0$ and $i<k$. For all $t\in\mathbb{N}$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:counter}
N_i(t)=\left\lfloor\frac{t}{R}\right\rfloor r_i+\min\bigl(r_i,\;(t\bmod R)-P_i\bigr)^{+},
\end{equation}
where $x^+=\max(x,0)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $t$; both sides vanish at $t=0$. For the step, $N_i(t+1)=N_i(t)+[\blk(t)=i]$ and $\blk(t)=i$ exactly when $P_i\le t\bmod R<P_i+r_i$. Writing $s=t\bmod R$: if $s<P_i$ the clamped term is $0$ and stays $0$; if $P_i\le s<P_i+r_i$ it increases by exactly $1$, matching the indicator; if $s\ge P_i+r_i$ it is saturated at $r_i$. At the wrap $s=R-1\mapsto 0$ the quotient $\lfloor t/R\rfloor$ increases by one, contributing exactly the $r_i$ that the clamped term releases. The identity $R\lfloor t/R\rfloor+(t\bmod R)=t$ and $P_i+r_i\le R$ close the case analysis.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exactness at period boundaries]\label{cor:period}
$N_i(nR)=n\,r_i$, hence $D_i(nR)=0$, for all $n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp two-sided discrepancy bound]\label{thm:disc}
For $R>0$, $i<k$ and all $t$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:disc}
-\,r_i P_i\;\le\;D_i(t)\;\le\;r_i\,(R-P_{i+1}),
\end{equation}
and both bounds are attained: $D_i(P_i)=-r_iP_i$ and $D_i(P_{i+1})=r_i(R-P_{i+1})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $t=Rq+s$, $0\le s<R$, and $c=\min(r_i,(s-P_i)^+)$, so \eqref{eq:counter} gives $N_i(t)=qr_i+c$ and
\[
D_i(t)=R(qr_i+c)-r_i(Rq+s)=R\,c-r_i\,s ,
\]
the complete periods cancelling exactly. If $s\le P_i$ then $c=0$ and $D_i(t)=-r_is\in[-r_iP_i,0]$. If $P_i\le s\le P_{i+1}$ then $c=s-P_i$ and $D_i(t)=(R-r_i)s-RP_i$, non-decreasing in $s$, hence between $-r_iP_i$ (at $s=P_i$) and $r_i(R-P_{i+1})$ (at $s=P_{i+1}$). If $s\ge P_{i+1}$ then $c=r_i$ and $D_i(t)=r_i(R-s)\in[0,r_i(R-P_{i+1})]$. Attainment follows from $N_i(P_i)=0$ and $N_i(P_{i+1})=r_i$.
\end{proof}

Thus \emph{a client's worst lag is its rate times the mass scheduled before it, and its worst lead is its rate times the mass scheduled after it}.

\begin{corollary}[Fairness constant]\label{cor:blockfair}
$|D_i(t)|\le r_i(R-r_i)$, so the block schedule is $B$-fair with $B=\max_{i<k}r_i(R-r_i)$.
\end{corollary}

For the balanced profile $(c,c)$ this gives $B=c^2$, attained, so the normalised discrepancy $c/2$ is unbounded.

\begin{theorem}[No starvation; sharp waiting window]\label{thm:wait}
If $r_i>0$ then for every $t$ there is $s$ with $t\le s<t+(R-r_i+1)$ and $\blk(s)=i$. The window cannot be shortened, as client $i$ is served in one contiguous run of length $r_i$ per period.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $s_0=t\bmod R$ and $q=\lfloor t/R\rfloor$: if $s_0<P_i$ take $s=Rq+P_i$; if $P_i\le s_0<P_{i+1}$ take $s=t$; otherwise take $s=R(q+1)+P_i$. In each case the displacement is at most $R-r_i$, the gap between consecutive service runs.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Round robin and self-similarity]\label{thm:rr}
(i) For $r\equiv 1$ on $k$ clients, $\blk(t)=t\bmod k$. (ii) For $m\ge 1$, $\blk_{mr}(t)=\blk_r(\lfloor t/m\rfloor)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(i) $P_i=i$, $R=k$, so $\beta(s)=s$. (ii) $P^{mr}_i=mP_i$, hence $P^{mr}_i\le s\iff P_i\le\lfloor s/m\rfloor$, and $\lfloor (t\bmod mR)/m\rfloor=\lfloor t/m\rfloor\bmod R$.
\end{proof}

\section{Impossibility and optimality}

\begin{theorem}[No exact schedule]\label{thm:noexact}
Let $k\ge 2$ with all rates positive. There is no schedule $f$ on $k$ clients with $R\,N^f_i(t)=r_it$ for all $i<k$ and all $t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $j=f(0)$; then $N^f_j(1)=1$ and exactness at $t=1$ forces $R=r_j$. But some $j'\ne j$ has $r_{j'}>0$ and $r_j+r_{j'}\le R$, so $r_j<R$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exactness exactly at period boundaries]\label{thm:iff}
Let $k\ge2$ with all rates positive. The block schedule is exact at $t$ if and only if $R\mid t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Sufficiency is Corollary~\ref{cor:period}. Conversely let $0<s=t\bmod R<R$. As in Theorem~\ref{thm:disc}, exactness for client $i$ reads $Rc_i=r_is$ with $c_i=\min(r_i,(s-P_i)^+)$. For a client whose block lies entirely before $s$ this forces $s=R$; for one lying entirely after $s$ it forces $s=0$. With $k\ge2$ some client other than $\beta(s)$ is of one of these two kinds, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Universal fairness lower bound]\label{thm:lower}
If $f$ is a schedule on $k$ clients that is $B$-fair for $(r,k)$ then $B\ge R-r_{f(0)}$; in particular $B\ge\min_{i<k}(R-r_i)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$N^f_{f(0)}(1)=1$, so $D_{f(0)}(1)=R-r_{f(0)}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Optimality of round robin]\label{cor:rropt}
For the uniform profile, round robin is $(k-1)$-fair, and every $B$-fair schedule on $k$ clients has $B\ge k-1$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Two clients: the Bresenham schedule}

\begin{definition}
For $0\le a\le R$, $R>0$, the \emph{Bresenham schedule} $\bres_{a,R}$ serves client $0$ in slot $t$ if $\lfloor ta/R\rfloor<\lfloor (t+1)a/R\rfloor$, and client $1$ otherwise. It is a schedule for the profile $(a,R-a)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Beatty counters]\label{prop:beatty}
$N_0(t)=\lfloor ta/R\rfloor$ and $N_1(t)=t-\lfloor ta/R\rfloor$ for all $t$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Unit discrepancy]\label{thm:bresfair}
$\bres_{a,R}$ is $(R-1)$-fair for $(a,R-a)$: $|R\,N_i(t)-r_i t|\le R-1$ for both clients and all $t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $ta=R\lfloor ta/R\rfloor+\rho$ with $0\le\rho\le R-1$. Then $R N_0(t)-at=-\rho$ and, since $N_1=t-N_0$ and $r_1=R-a$, $RN_1(t)-(R-a)t=\rho$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Separation from the block schedule]\label{thm:sep}
For $c\ge2$ and the profile $(c,c)$ (so $R=2c$), $\bres_{c,2c}$ is $(R-1)$-fair while the block schedule has $D_0(c)=c^2>R-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp waiting bounds]\label{thm:breswait}
For $0<a\le R$, client $0$ is served at least once in every window of $\lceil R/a\rceil$ consecutive slots; for $a<R$, client $1$ is served at least once in every window of $\lceil R/(R-a)\rceil$ slots.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $g=\lceil R/a\rceil$ then $ga\ge R$, so $\lfloor (t+g)a/R\rfloor\ge\lfloor ta/R\rfloor+1$; an increase of the counter forces a service inside the window. The other case is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\section{The nested-floor obstruction for $k\ge3$}

Since two-client counters are differences of floors, one is tempted to set
\begin{equation}\label{eq:nested}
\widehat N_i(t)=\left\lfloor\frac{t\,P_{i+1}}{R}\right\rfloor-\left\lfloor\frac{t\,P_i}{R}\right\rfloor .
\end{equation}
These have the right long-run frequencies and telescope to $\sum_i\widehat N_i(t)=t$.

\begin{proposition}
For $k=2$ and the profile $(a,R-a)$, $\widehat N_i(t)=N^{\bres}_i(t)$ for $i\in\{0,1\}$ and all $t$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Obstruction]\label{thm:obstruction}
There is a profile with $k=3$ and all rates positive for which no schedule $f$ satisfies $N^f_i(t)=\widehat N_i(t)$ for all $i<3$ and all $t$. Explicitly, for $r=(3,1,3)$ (so $R=7$, $P=(0,3,4,7)$),
\[
\widehat N_1(2)=\left\lfloor\tfrac{8}{7}\right\rfloor-\left\lfloor\tfrac{6}{7}\right\rfloor=1,\qquad
\widehat N_1(3)=\left\lfloor\tfrac{12}{7}\right\rfloor-\left\lfloor\tfrac{9}{7}\right\rfloor=0 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Counters of schedules are non-decreasing; these values decrease.
\end{proof}

Conceptually, $\widehat N_i$ counts jumps of the Beatty sequence of slope $P_{i+1}/R$ minus those of slope $P_i/R$; a simultaneous jump makes the difference fall. Whether such collisions occur is an arithmetic property of $(P_i,P_{i+1},R)$.

\section{Splitting trees: logarithmic discrepancy for every profile}

\begin{definition}[Splitting tree]
A \emph{splitting tree} is a finite binary tree $T::=\mathrm{leaf}(i,w)\mid \mathrm{node}(T_\ell,T_r)$ with weight $\wt(\mathrm{leaf}(i,w))=w$, $\wt(\mathrm{node})=\wt(T_\ell)+\wt(T_r)$; depth $\dep(\mathrm{leaf})=0$, $\dep(\mathrm{node})=1+\max(\dep T_\ell,\dep T_r)$; label set $\lab(\mathrm{leaf}(i,w))=\{i\}$, $\lab(\mathrm{node})=\lab(T_\ell)\cup\lab(T_r)$; and label rate $\mathrm{rate}_T(j)=w[j=i]$ at a leaf, summed at nodes. $T$ is \emph{well formed} if all leaf rates are positive and the label sets of siblings are disjoint.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Recursive Bresenham schedule of a tree]
$\sch_{\mathrm{leaf}(i,w)}(t)=i$, and with $A=\wt(T_\ell)$, $B=\wt(T_r)$, $W=A+B$,
\[
\sch_{\mathrm{node}(T_\ell,T_r)}(t)=
\begin{cases}
\sch_{T_\ell}\!\left(\left\lfloor tA/W\right\rfloor\right), & \bres_{A,W}(t)=0,\\[2pt]
\sch_{T_r}\!\left(t-\left\lfloor tA/W\right\rfloor\right), & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

The two-client rule with rates $(A,B)$ decides which subtree owns the slot, and the chosen subtree sees it on its own clock: by Proposition~\ref{prop:beatty} the left subtree's local time $\lfloor tA/W\rfloor$ is exactly the number of slots it has received. Consequently the counters compose,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:compose}
N^{\sch_T}_i(t)=N^{\sch_{T_\ell}}_i\!\left(\left\lfloor tA/W\right\rfloor\right)\qquad (i\in\lab(T_\ell)),
\end{equation}
and symmetrically on the right; \eqref{eq:compose} is the technical heart of the construction.

\begin{theorem}[Splitting-tree discrepancy]\label{thm:tree}
Let $T$ be a well-formed splitting tree of weight $W$. For every label $i$ and every $t$,
\[
\bigl|W\,N^{\sch_T}_i(t)-\mathrm{rate}_T(i)\,t\bigr|\;\le\;W\cdot\dep(T);
\]
that is, the normalised discrepancy of $\sch_T$ is at most $\dep(T)$ --- a bound depending only on the shape of the tree, never on the rates.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $T$. At a leaf the discrepancy is $0$. At a node with $A=\wt(T_\ell)$, $B=\wt(T_r)$, $W=A+B$, take $i\in\lab(T_\ell)$, set $m=\lfloor tA/W\rfloor$, $w=\mathrm{rate}_{T_\ell}(i)$, $c=N^{\sch_{T_\ell}}_i(m)$. Theorem~\ref{thm:bresfair} gives $|Wm-At|\le W-1$ (the node is fair to the whole left subtree), and the induction hypothesis gives $|Ac-wm|\le A\dep(T_\ell)$. The exact identity
\[
A\,(Wc-wt)=W\,(Ac-wm)+w\,(Wm-At)
\]
together with $w\le A$ yields $A|Wc-wt|\le WA\dep(T_\ell)+A(W-1)$, i.e.
$|Wc-wt|\le W\dep(T_\ell)+(W-1)<W(\dep(T_\ell)+1)\le W\dep(T)$. One splitting level therefore costs strictly less than one unit of normalised discrepancy, and the errors accumulate additively along a root-to-leaf path.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Balanced trees]
For a rate function $w$, $\bal(w,\mathrm{base},1)=\mathrm{leaf}(\mathrm{base},w_{\mathrm{base}})$ and, for $n\ge2$,
\[
\bal(w,\mathrm{base},n)=\mathrm{node}\bigl(\bal(w,\mathrm{base},\lfloor n/2\rfloor),\,\bal(w,\mathrm{base}+\lfloor n/2\rfloor,\lceil n/2\rceil)\bigr).
\]
The \emph{perfect} tree over $2^d$ clients splits exactly in half at every node.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:bal}
If $w_{\mathrm{base}},\dots,w_{\mathrm{base}+n-1}>0$ then $\bal(w,\mathrm{base},n)$ is well formed, has label set $\{\mathrm{base},\dots,\mathrm{base}+n-1\}$, weight $\sum_{j}w_j$ over that range, label rates agreeing with $w$, and $\dep\le\lceil\log_2 n\rceil$; the perfect tree over $2^d$ clients has depth exactly $d$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Logarithmic fairness for every profile]\label{thm:log}
For every $k\ge1$ and every positive rate profile $r$, the schedule $\sch_{\bal(r,0,k)}$ is $B$-fair with $B=R\lceil\log_2 k\rceil$: its normalised discrepancy is at most $\lceil\log_2 k\rceil$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
Three or four clients with arbitrary positive rates admit an explicit schedule of normalised discrepancy at most $2$; $2^d$ clients admit one of normalised discrepancy at most $d$.
\end{corollary}

The contrast with Section~3 is stark: the block schedule's normalised discrepancy is $\Theta(R)$ already for $(c,c)$, whereas the tree bound depends on $k$ alone.

\section{The online largest-lag greedy rule}

\begin{definition}[Greedy largest-lag]
Given counters $N_i(t)$, serve at slot $t$ a client maximising $\obj_i(t)=r_i(t+1)-R\,N_i(t)$, ties broken by smallest index. Write $\lag_i(t)=r_it-RN_i(t)=-D_i(t)$, so $\obj_i(t)=\lag_i(t)+r_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Zero-sum identities]\label{lem:zerosum}
$\sum_{i<k}\lag_i(t)=0$ and $\sum_{i<k}\obj_i(t)=R$ for all $t$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[The served client is owed a unit]\label{lem:owed}
If $R>0$ and $k\ge1$ then $\obj_{p(t)}(t)\ge1$, where $p(t)$ is the greedy choice.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The $k$ objective values sum to $R\ge1$, so their maximum is at least $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No client ever leads by a full period]\label{thm:greedylead}
For every profile with $R>0$ and every $i,t$: $\lag_i(t)\ge-(R-1)$, i.e. $D_i(t)\le R-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $t$; all lags vanish at $t=0$. Since $\lag_i(t+1)=\obj_i(t)-R[i=p(t)]$: for $i\ne p(t)$, $\lag_i(t+1)=\lag_i(t)+r_i\ge\lag_i(t)$; for $i=p(t)$, $\lag_i(t+1)=\obj_i(t)-R\ge1-R$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:owed}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Greedy is $(k-1)(R-1)$-fair]\label{thm:greedyfair}
For $k\ge2$, $R>0$, all $i<k$ and all $t$: $\lag_i(t)\le(k-1)(R-1)$, hence $|D_i(t)|\le(k-1)(R-1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\lag_i(t)=-\sum_{j\ne i}\lag_j(t)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:zerosum}, and each of the $k-1$ terms is $\ge-(R-1)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:greedylead}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Separation from the block schedule]
For $(c,c)$ with $c\ge2$, greedy satisfies $D_i(t)\le R-1$ for all $i,t$, whereas the block schedule has $D_0(c)=c^2>R-1$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Greedy is not unit-fair}

Theorem~\ref{thm:greedylead} is the lead side of a would-be unit-fairness statement; the natural conjecture is that $\lag_i(t)\le R-1$ as well, the multi-client analogue of Theorem~\ref{thm:bresfair}. An exhaustive sweep of primitive profiles with $k\le6$ and rates $\le6$ mostly supports it. The conjecture is nevertheless false. Direct simulation on
\[
r=(1,1,1,5,5,5),\qquad R=18,\qquad k=6,
\]
gives $N(11)=(1,1,1,3,3,2)$ and hence
\[
\lag_5(11)=5\cdot11-18\cdot2=19>18=R,
\]
a normalised lag of $19/18\approx1.056$ (the proved lead bound $D_i(t)\le17$ holds throughout). The failure is systematic: for the family $r=(1^m,c^m)$ the observed normalised discrepancy increases monotonically in $m$ and $c$ --- $1.056$ at $(m,c)=(3,5)$, $1.25$ at $(6,9)$, $1.325$ at $(12,9)$, $1.422$ at $(20,50)$, $1.438$ at $(30,50)$ --- approaching but apparently never reaching $3/2$. The mechanism: many cheap unit-rate clients bias the objective towards the heavy clients, which then round-robin among themselves, letting the last heavy client slip nearly two heavy-cycles behind before the objective corrects.

\begin{conjecture}[Universal constant for greedy]\label{conj:greedy}
There is an absolute constant $C$ --- conjecturally $C=3/2$, not attained --- such that for every $k$, every positive rate profile and every $t$, the greedy largest-lag schedule satisfies $|D_i(t)|<C\,R$.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Algorithms and complexity}

\paragraph{(A) Block schedule by prefix sums.} Precompute $P_0,\dots,P_k$ in $O(k)$. To answer ``who owns slot $t$?'', compute $s=t\bmod R$ and binary search for $i$ with $P_i\le s<P_{i+1}$: $O(\log k)$ per slot, $O(k)$ memory. Counters are available in $O(1)$ from \eqref{eq:counter}, so the schedule supports random access into the arbitrarily distant future.

\paragraph{(B) Splitting-tree schedule.} Build the balanced tree in $O(k)$ with subtree weights. To answer a query, walk from the root: at a node with weights $(A,B)$ and local time $\tau$, compute $m=\lfloor\tau A/(A+B)\rfloor$ and $m'=\lfloor(\tau+1)A/(A+B)\rfloor$; if $m<m'$ descend left with local time $m$, else descend right with local time $\tau-m$. Cost $O(\log k)$ per slot, no history needed. Guarantee: normalised discrepancy $\le\lceil\log_2k\rceil$.

\paragraph{(C) Greedy largest-lag.} Maintain counters and select $\arg\max_i(r_i(t+1)-RN_i(t))$: $O(k)$ per slot naively, or $O(\log k)$ with a priority queue keyed by virtual finish times. Guarantees: $D_i(t)\le R-1$ and $|D_i(t)|\le(k-1)(R-1)$; stateful, so no random access.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
Schedule & Exact at & Normalised discrepancy & Random access\\
\midrule
Block (prefix sums) & multiples of $R$ only & $\max_i r_i(R-r_i)/R$, i.e.\ $\Theta(R)$ & yes\\
Splitting tree & multiples of $R$ & $\le\lceil\log_2 k\rceil$, rate-independent & yes\\
Greedy & multiples of $R$ & lead $<1$; lag $\le k-1$ proved, $\approx1.44$ observed & no\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Applications}

\textbf{Packet scheduling.} With $r_i$ the weight of flow $i$, $D_i(t)$ is the service lag of the deficit-round-robin literature. Theorem~\ref{thm:disc} quantifies why per-flow quantum cycling is bursty: the burst equals $r_iP_i$. Theorem~\ref{thm:log} shows a hierarchical scheduler bounds burstiness by $\lceil\log_2k\rceil$ packets regardless of weight skew.

\textbf{Mixed-model assembly lines.} For demands $r_i$ over a shift of $R$ units, $|D_i(t)|/R$ is the maximum-deviation objective of product rate variation. Theorem~\ref{thm:noexact} says it cannot vanish, Theorem~\ref{thm:lower} bounds it below, and Theorem~\ref{thm:log} gives a constructive logarithmic guarantee.

\textbf{Apportionment.} With $t$ a house size and $r_i$ populations, $|D_i(t)|<R$ is exactly ``staying within quota''. Theorem~\ref{thm:greedylead} says largest-lag never exceeds the upper quota, while Section~8.1 exhibits populations violating the lower quota.

\textbf{Real-time scheduling.} For periodic tasks the pfair criterion is $|D_i(t)|<R$; Theorem~\ref{thm:greedylead} supplies one half of it for free, and splitting trees give a deterministic precomputable schedule with logarithmic worst-case error.

\textbf{Stride scheduling.} Stride scheduling (smallest virtual time first) is greedy in disguise; the counterexample of Section~8.1 shows that the folklore ``within one service'' claim fails for $k\ge6$ with two-scale weights.

\section{Discussion and open problems}

Three principles emerge. \emph{Prefix sums are the right coordinates}: the batch decomposition, the closed form, the sharp bounds, the waiting window and the nested-floor obstruction are all statements about the position of $t\bmod R$ relative to the cut points $P_i$. \emph{Exactness and smoothness are inequivalent, quantifiably}: no schedule is exact always, the most exact schedule pays $\Theta(R)$, and the smooth schedules gain exactness only at period boundaries. \emph{Fairness is a property of decomposition, not of arithmetic}: the $\lceil\log_2k\rceil$ bound needs no hypotheses because the construction halves the client set rather than manipulating rates.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{The constant for greedy} (Conjecture~\ref{conj:greedy}). The lead side is settled; the lag side is the negated sum of the other leads, so the whole problem is to quantify the cancellation among leads. A potential function on the sorted lag vector is the natural tool, with $(1^m,c^m)$ the extremal candidate.
\item \textbf{Normalised discrepancy $<1$ for all profiles?} True for $k=2$; our best constructive general bound is $\lceil\log_2k\rceil$. Is there always a schedule with $|D_i(t)|\le R-1$?
\item \textbf{Classification of nested-floor schedulability.} A drop of $\widehat N_i$ is exactly a simultaneous increment of the Beatty sequences of slopes $P_i/R$ and $P_{i+1}/R$; realisability should be equivalent to an arithmetic non-collision condition on $\gcd(P_i,R)$ and the residues of $P_is\bmod R$ for $s$ in a prescribed interval.
\item \textbf{Optimal tree shapes.} Theorem~\ref{thm:tree} charges one unit per level, so minimising \emph{weighted} depth (Huffman-style, heavy clients near the root) should beat the balanced tree for skewed profiles. Can $\lceil\log_2k\rceil$ be replaced by something like the entropy of $r/R$?
\item \textbf{Lower bounds beyond the first slot.} Theorem~\ref{thm:lower} uses only $t=1$; what is the true optimum for a given profile? For the uniform profile it is $(k-1)/k$.
\item \textbf{Multi-resource and dynamic profiles.} The closed form extends to $m$ identical servers by scaling time; the behaviour when clients arrive and depart (so $R$ changes) is open even for $k=2$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Summary of results}

\begin{itemize}
\item Prefix sums cut $\{0,\dots,R-1\}$ into disjoint batches of sizes exactly $r_0,\dots,r_{k-1}$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:batches}).
\item Closed-form counter $N_i(t)=\lfloor t/R\rfloor r_i+\min(r_i,(t\bmod R)-P_i)^+$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:counter}).
\item Sharp bounds $-r_iP_i\le D_i(t)\le r_i(R-P_{i+1})$, both attained (Theorem~\ref{thm:disc}); fairness constant $\max_i r_i(R-r_i)$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:blockfair}).
\item Exactness at all multiples of $R$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:period}) and only there (Theorem~\ref{thm:iff}); no schedule is exact always (Theorem~\ref{thm:noexact}).
\item Service in every window of $R-r_i+1$ slots, sharp (Theorem~\ref{thm:wait}); round robin optimal for the uniform profile (Corollary~\ref{cor:rropt}).
\item Two clients: Bresenham is $(R-1)$-fair (Theorem~\ref{thm:bresfair}) with sharp windows $\lceil R/r_i\rceil$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:breswait}) and beats the block schedule on $(c,c)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:sep}).
\item The nested-floor generalisation is unrealisable for $k\ge3$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:obstruction}).
\item Splitting trees: normalised discrepancy $\le\dep(T)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:tree}), hence $\le\lceil\log_2k\rceil$ for every profile (Theorem~\ref{thm:log}).
\item Greedy: $D_i(t)\le R-1$ always (Theorem~\ref{thm:greedylead}) and $(k-1)(R-1)$-fair (Theorem~\ref{thm:greedyfair}), but not unit-fair --- $(1,1,1,5,5,5)$ reaches lag $19>R=18$.
\end{itemize}

